<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		Privacy manifest for TripPlanner.

		Required by Apple since Spring 2024 for every app and SDK submitted to
		the App Store. This file is the app's own declaration; each third-party
		SDK must ship its own alongside its binary, and Capacitor's packages
		do (they arrive via SPM into the built product, which is why none are
		found loose in this repo).

		THE TRAP IS THE COMMENTS, NOT THE TYPES.

		A double hyphen is ILLEGAL anywhere inside an XML comment. This file
		previously used several as prose dashes, and Apple's strict XML parser
		rejected the entire manifest with ITMS-91056 ("Keys and values in your
		app's privacy manifests must be valid") the moment it met one.

		Why it took two rejections to find: `plutil -lint` reports OK on those
		files, because plutil is lenient about comment bodies. So the one check
		anyone would naturally run says the file is fine while Apple rejects it.

		Never write two hyphens in a row inside a comment here. Use an em dash
		or a single hyphen. Verify with a STRICT XML parse, never plutil:

		    python3 -c "import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET; \
		      ET.parse('ios/App/App/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy'); print('ok')"

		On types, for the record, they are UNIFORM: NSPrivacyTracking at the
		top level and both NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked and
		NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking inside each entry are BOOLEANS.
		Apple documents all three as such. (An earlier revision of this file
		claimed the inner two were strings; that was wrong and is what build 2
		shipped. Both builds were rejected for the comment hyphens instead.)

		Everything here is a statement of fact about what the app actually
		does. It was checked against the source rather than filled in from a
		template, because an inaccurate manifest is worse than a missing one:
		it is a signed declaration, and reviewers do diff it against observed
		behaviour.

		Verified absences, which are why the corresponding keys are NOT here:
		  * No geolocation. Nothing in src/ calls navigator.geolocation. Maps
		    are rendered from stored trip data, not the device's position.
		  * No camera, microphone, or photo library access.
		  * No notification permission requests.
		  * No ATT / IDFA / advertising identifiers, and no analytics SDK.
		    The app talks to exactly three kinds of host: its own origin, the
		    Open-Meteo weather/geocoding APIs, and OpenStreetMap/Nominatim for
		    geocoding and map tiles. None are ad or tracking networks.
	-->

	<!--
		No tracking. The app does not link user or device data with data from
		third parties for advertising, and does not share data with data
		brokers. A false value here is the strong claim, and it is true: there
		is no ad SDK and no cross-app identifier in the binary.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
		Empty, and deliberately explicit rather than omitted, so the absence of
		tracking domains is a positive declaration a reviewer can read at a
		glance instead of having to infer.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
		Collected data types.

		TripPlanner stores an account email, a display name, and the travel
		itinerary the user enters (trips, bookings, notes). Two properties
		matter for the answers below:

		  * It is all first-party. The data is written to the operator's own
		    SQLite instance. It is not sent to a third party for processing,
		    so the purposes are App Functionality only: not Analytics, not
		    Third-Party Advertising, not Developer Advertising.
		  * All of it is user-provided. The user types their email, their
		    name, and their bookings. Nothing is collected passively from the
		    device, which is why no NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeSensor or
		    *UsageData / *Diagnostics entries appear.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>

		<!-- Account identity: email + display name, supplied at sign-up. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<!-- Both booleans, like the top-level NSPrivacyTracking. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!-- Display name. Same handling as the email above. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
			Itinerary content: flight numbers, hotel names and addresses, car
			rentals, notes. Apple has no "travel" category, so this is other
			user content, the residual bucket for data that is not identity,
			financial, or device telemetry. It is linked because it belongs to
			the account that created it.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

	</array>

	<!--
		Required-reason APIs.

		This app calls none of them from its own code: no UserDefaults, no
		file-timestamp or disk-space probes, no system-boot-time reads, no
		active-keyboard queries. The list is therefore empty.

		It is empty on purpose rather than by omission. If a future change adds
		a Capacitor plugin that reads UserDefaults, that plugin ships its own
		manifest declaring its own reason; this file does not need to grow for
		that. It would need to grow only if app-authored Swift started calling
		one of those APIs directly.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>

</dict>
</plist>
